# @io/algebra — the algebra-altitude @io boundary species.
#
# Sibling to @io/cargo (the Rust build-system delegate, 2026-06-05),
# @io/oci (the OCI Distribution adapter, 2026-06-24), @io/stagefreight
# (the wire-protocol surface, 2026-06-23), @io/git (the git plumbing
# delegate) at the @io species altitude. Same shape — typed contract
# the mirror side sees, opaque non-mirror body discharging at the
# realisation boundary — at the algebra altitude.
#
# === The recognition (#104 chain P7, 2026-06-30) ===
#
# @algebra (P6, `34cf333`) lifted A — the typed operation surface of
# the Connes spectral triple — to family-root altitude as substrate-
# decl. That admission named WHAT an algebra IS at substrate altitude
# (carrier, operation_set, composition rule). What was still missing:
# the @io-boundary species that exposes substrate-altitude algebras
# to external consumers AND consumes foreign-language algebra
# libraries back into substrate altitude.
#
# This shard lands the BOUNDARY species for algebras. Same family
# pattern as @io/cargo for Rust builds, @io/oci for container
# distribution, @io/stagefreight for crystal wire transport: name
# the typed surface where substrate-altitude content meets the
# non-mirror world for ONE specific boundary kind. The boundary
# kind here is the algebra altitude.
#
# === The fold-back terminal ===
#
# Per the P8 forward-promise threaded across @glue (`8d3f89e` §"The
# self-recursive use case forward-promise") and the @fate × @glue ×
# @kintsugi composition documented in `docs/specs/bauchladen-
# autopoietic-fate.md` §4.5: the substrate's self-recursive boot
# collapse composes
#
#   @glue × @kintsugi × @fate → @io/algebra
#
# at P8. @io/algebra IS THE OUTPUT ALTITUDE of that composition.
# The three composed prisms each contribute:
#
#   - @kintsugi emits per-step candidate morphisms (the
#     transformation engine's per-step output)
#   - @glue lifts each candidate as a typed correspondence between
#     spectral triples (the morphism-category vocabulary)
#   - @fate selects one morphism within each correspondence's
#     restriction (the constrained-inference operator)
#
# The composition's OUTPUT — the settled translated algebra-payload
# crossing the substrate's outermost boundary — lands in @io/algebra's
# typed surface as an `io_algebra_exposure` crystal. The fold-back
# terminal IS the operational form of the substrate "closing its own
# tail" (per Glint 2026-06-23's framing; the operational form of the
# substrate-decl-on-Nix-on-OCI-on-substrate ouroboros named at
# @io/oci).
#
# This shard lands P7 — the substrate-decl admission that names the
# terminal altitude. P8 will land the composition discharge (the
# operational composition of @glue × @kintsugi × @fate against the
# typed surface this shard declares).
#
# === @io/algebra IS BOTH a @io species AND a @glue species ===
#
# Structurally load-bearing fact: @io/algebra inherits from TWO
# substrate family-roots:
#
#   - `in @io` — the boundary-with-non-mirror-world discipline.
#     Inherits @io's `imperfect<a, e, l>` return shape on every
#     boundary action; inherits the substrate-only-legitimate-non-
#     mirror-surface discipline; inherits the partition between
#     permanent-@io content (algebras whose realisation will always
#     live outside substrate altitude) and cross-wall-kintsugi
#     candidates (algebras whose realisation may fold into substrate
#     altitude when analyzability matures).
#
#   - `in @glue` — the morphism-category discipline. @io/algebra's
#     `translate` action IS-A @glue species action at the io-algebra
#     altitude. The translation across the @io boundary (substrate
#     algebra ↔ external algebra) IS a correspondence in Mesland's
#     category (per `shards/glue.mirror` §"The three known species
#     of @glue"). This shard adds @io/algebra as the FOURTH known
#     @glue species, sibling to:
#
#       Species                            Altitude               Substrate evidence
#       --------------------------------   --------------------   --------------------
#       @cascade/code/<src>/<tgt>          code-translation       shards/cascade.mirror
#                                                                  (LANDED; @glue
#                                                                   inheritance edge
#                                                                   forward-promised)
#       @cascade/code/formal/prose         formal/prose           `437d061` (LANDED)
#       @spectral/metalogue/tomm           spectral-metalogue     docs/specs/spectral-
#                                                                  metalogue.md `16f4564`
#                                                                  (SPEC LANDED; shard
#                                                                   FORWARD-PROMISED
#                                                                   per Seam audit C-3
#                                                                   closure 2026-06-30)
#       @io/algebra (THIS shard)           io-algebra             shards/io/algebra.mirror
#                                                                  (LANDED)
#
# The dual inheritance is structurally honest: the @io/algebra
# boundary IS a translation surface (consumes/exposes algebras across
# the @io wall) AND IS an @io boundary species (typed contract with
# the non-mirror world). Both family-roots' obligations compose
# transitively; the shard discharges both via the inheritance
# predicate at §4.
#
# === Use cases ===
#
# Two structurally-symmetric use cases the family-root names:
#
#   1. External consumers reading substrate-altitude algebras.
#      A non-mirror consumer (Rust client, Python notebook, foreign
#      language library) needs to read a substrate-altitude algebra
#      (e.g., the canonical five-op algebra of a prism, or the
#      C*-algebra a `@algebra/c_star` species exposes). The
#      `expose` action declared below lifts the algebra to an
#      `io_algebra_exposure` crystal; `address` content-addresses
#      the exposure via @bauchladen; downstream @io species (OCI,
#      stagefreight) ship the addressed crystal to the consumer.
#
#   2. Foreign algebra libraries entering substrate altitude.
#      An external algebra source (a Python sympy module, a Rust
#      `num-traits` definition, a Lean4 algebraic structure) needs
#      to be consumed AS an `algebra_carrier` at substrate altitude
#      so downstream prisms can `@algebra.enumerate_operations` /
#      `@algebra.compose` against it. The `consume` action declared
#      below crosses the @io boundary and crystallizes the external
#      algebra as a substrate-altitude `algebra_carrier`. The
#      translation respects the cross-wall-kintsugi candidacy
#      partition (per @io family-root): foreign algebras whose
#      operations are byte-level pure (group multiplication tables,
#      finite ring presentations) are cross-wall-kintsugi candidates;
#      foreign algebras whose operations depend on external state
#      (vendor SDK calls, kernel syscalls) remain permanent-@io.
#
# === Relationship to @algebra family-root ===
#
# @algebra (the family-root) declares WHAT an algebra IS at substrate
# altitude. @io/algebra (this shard) declares HOW substrate-altitude
# algebras MEET the non-mirror world. The relationship is the
# canonical (family-root) ↔ (@io-altitude-exposure) pattern:
#
#   - @mirror/store ↔ @io/git    storage family-root ↔ git plumbing
#   - @algebra      ↔ @io/algebra algebra family-root ↔ this shard
#   - @cascade      ↔ @io/oci    cascade family ↔ OCI distribution
#                                  (artifacts ship through OCI; the
#                                   cascade-built outputs land in
#                                   the OCI registry)
#
# Each family-root names a substrate-altitude discipline; the @io
# species names the boundary where that discipline crosses to the
# non-mirror world. The two families partition the substrate's
# expressible surface for the algebra discipline: @algebra declares
# operations the substrate KNOWS about; @io/algebra declares the
# boundary the substrate USES to exchange algebras with the world.
#
# === Connes (A, H, D) at the @io boundary ===
#
# Per `architecture-connes-spectral-triple`: the substrate IS the
# operational form of Connes' real spectral triple (A, H, D, J, γ).
# @algebra (P6) named A at family-root altitude. The @io/algebra
# boundary species names WHERE A MEETS THE WORLD — the @io-altitude
# exposure of A across the substrate's outermost wall.
#
# In Connes' terms: A is the algebra of observables; H is the state
# space they act on; D drives the spectral flow. @io/algebra declares
# the boundary at which substrate-altitude A becomes addressable to
# (and consumable from) external A's. The mathematical content is
# the Mesland correspondence at the @io altitude — the morphism
# between the substrate's A and the external world's A — which
# composes structurally with @glue's correspondence vocabulary.
#
# === Pre-AI prior art ===
#
#   Foreign function interface  Bell Labs (1970s); the canonical
#                                discipline of crossing a language
#                                boundary while preserving typed
#                                contract. SWIG (1996), ctypes (2003),
#                                CFFI (2012) are the modern
#                                instances. @io/algebra IS the
#                                substrate-altitude FFI for the
#                                algebra altitude.
#
#   Computer algebra systems   Macsyma (1968), Maple (1980), Sage
#                                (2005), SymPy (2007). External
#                                algebra libraries the substrate may
#                                consume via @io/algebra; each
#                                exposes algebraic structures as
#                                consumable surfaces.
#
#   ATLAS of Lie groups        Adams-du Cloux 2007; reference
#                                database of Lie algebra structure
#                                consumable as an external algebra
#                                source per the use case 2 pattern.
#
#   Open OnDemand              Hudak et al. 2018; the canonical
#                                @io-altitude exposure pattern for
#                                computational artifacts. Sibling
#                                discipline to @io/oci's distribution
#                                shape at the artifact altitude.
#
#   Universal algebra          Birkhoff 1935 ("On the structure of
#                                abstract algebras", *Proc. Camb.
#                                Phil. Soc.* 31:433-454); the
#                                discipline of algebra-as-typed-
#                                surface that @io/algebra exposes
#                                across the @io wall.
#
#   Mesland correspondence     Mesland 2013 (arXiv:1304.3802);
#                                inherited via `in @glue` — the
#                                morphism-category vocabulary
#                                @io/algebra uses at the io-algebra
#                                altitude.
#
# Spec context: docs/specs/bauchladen-autopoietic-fate.md §4.5
#               (the @glue × @fate composition pattern; @io/algebra
#               IS-A @glue species and discharges this pattern at
#               the io-algebra altitude);
#               docs/specs/bauchladen-autopoietic-fate.md §6+ (the
#               #104 chain spec — the @io/algebra section names the
#               fold-back terminal altitude this shard declares);
#               docs/insights/2026-05-26-glass-wall-and-cross-wall-
#               kintsugi.md (the partition between permanent-@io
#               sub-species and cross-wall-kintsugi candidates;
#               @io/algebra inherits this partition for algebras).
